<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/interior.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/interior-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/interior.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Answers</span></nav><p class="kicker">Answers</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Questions from Maine Owners</h1><p class="lede">25 plain answers grouped by topic: the basics, cost and timing, Maine rules, winter and storm claims, and working with us.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><nav class="jump" aria-label="FAQ topics"><a href="#faq-0">Appraisal basics</a><a href="#faq-1">Cost, timing and paperwork</a><a href="#faq-2">Maine rules and resources</a><a href="#faq-3">Winter and storm claims</a><a href="#faq-4">Working with our practice</a></nav><h2 id="faq-0">Appraisal basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is insurance appraisal in plain terms?</summary><p>It is a process in most property policies for settling a disagreement over how much a covered loss costs. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and an award signed by two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p></details><details><summary>When does appraisal make sense for a homeowner?</summary><p>It makes sense when the carrier has accepted the claim but its estimate is well below what repairs will really cost, and talking with the adjuster has stalled. It is not designed for denied claims or arguments over whether something is covered.</p></details><details><summary>Can my insurance company demand appraisal too?</summary><p>Yes, many policies let either party invoke appraisal. If the carrier demands it, you will still name your own appraiser and the process runs the same way.</p></details><details><summary>Is an appraisal award final?</summary><p>Under most policy wording, an award signed by any two panel members is binding on the amount of loss. It can sometimes be challenged in court for reasons like fraud or a panel member who was not disinterested, which is a question for an attorney.</p></details><details><summary>How is appraisal different from a lawsuit?</summary><p>Appraisal decides only the dollar value of a covered loss and is usually faster and less formal than litigation. A lawsuit can address coverage, contract breaches and claim-handling conduct, which appraisal does not.</p></details></div><h2 id="faq-1">Cost, timing and paperwork</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays for the appraisers and the umpire?</summary><p>Most policies say each side pays its own appraiser and the two sides split the umpire’s fee equally. Check the appraisal condition in your policy, since some forms differ.</p></details><details><summary>How long does a typical appraisal take?</summary><p>Many residential appraisals wrap up within a few weeks to a few months after both appraisers are named. Winter access, umpire availability and the number of disputed items all affect the timeline.</p></details><details><summary>What documents should I gather before demanding appraisal?</summary><p>Collect the full policy with declarations and endorsements, every carrier estimate and payment letter, your contractor estimates, dated photos and any reports from mitigation companies or engineers. A simple timeline of calls and emails with the adjuster also helps.</p></details><details><summary>Do I still have to file a proof of loss?</summary><p>If your policy requires a sworn proof of loss, the requirement usually stays in place during appraisal. Read the deadline in your policy and meet it, and ask a Maine attorney if you are unsure how to complete it.</p></details><details><summary>Can I keep negotiating with the adjuster during appraisal?</summary><p>Yes, nothing usually stops the parties from settling on their own at any point. Many files close during the appraisers’ reconciliation without the umpire ever ruling.</p></details></div><h2 id="faq-2">Maine rules and resources</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who regulates insurance companies in Maine?</summary><p>The Maine Bureau of Insurance, part of the Department of Professional and Financial Regulation, oversees insurers and is headed by the Superintendent of Insurance. The Bureau accepts consumer complaints about claim handling.</p></details><details><summary>Does Maine have a FAIR plan for hard-to-insure homes?</summary><p>No. Maine chose a market-assistance approach instead of a FAIR plan or state wind pool, so many older, vacant or oceanfront homes are insured through surplus lines carriers whose policy wording can differ from standard forms.</p></details><details><summary>What Maine law covers unfair claim handling?</summary><p>Maine’s Unfair Claims Settlement Practices statute, 24-A M.R.S. section 2436-A, allows an insured to bring a civil action over certain claim-handling conduct by their own insurer. Courts apply it narrowly, and whether it fits your situation is a question for a Maine attorney, not an appraisal panel.</p></details><details><summary>Does my homeowners policy cover flood damage?</summary><p>Standard homeowners policies exclude flood and storm surge. Flood coverage comes from a separate National Flood Insurance Program or private flood policy, and appraisal under a homeowners policy will not change that exclusion.</p></details><details><summary>Is there a deadline to sue my insurance company in Maine?</summary><p>Most policies include a suit limitation clause, and many set a period of one to two years from the date of loss. Read your own policy and talk with a Maine attorney about how that clause and Maine law apply to your claim.</p></details></div><h2 id="faq-3">Winter and storm claims</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are ice dam leaks covered by homeowners insurance?</summary><p>Interior water damage from an ice dam is commonly covered, though policies often exclude the cost of the ice dam itself or repairs tied to wear and maintenance. The scope of the interior damage is where many ice dam disputes land.</p></details><details><summary>What if pipes froze at my seasonal camp while nobody was there?</summary><p>Many policies require you to keep heat on or drain the plumbing in an unoccupied building, and some limit coverage after a set number of days of vacancy. Review those conditions closely, since they may decide coverage before appraisal is even an option.</p></details><details><summary>How do adjusters split wind and water after a nor’easter?</summary><p>Wind and wind-driven rain entering through storm-created openings are usually covered, while rising water and storm surge fall under the flood exclusion. When both happen at once, the dispute often turns on which damage came from which source.</p></details><details><summary>Will my policy pay to remove a fallen tree?</summary><p>Policies typically pay to remove a tree that damaged a covered structure, often with a dollar cap per tree and per loss. A tree that fell without hitting anything covered may not qualify for removal coverage.</p></details><details><summary>Can a winter roof inspection be accurate?</summary><p>An inspection under snow can miss lifted shingles, cracked flashing and soft decking. If the roof is buried, it is reasonable to ask for a follow-up inspection after the thaw before the scope is finalized.</p></details></div><h2 id="faq-4">Working with our practice</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How are your fees set?</summary><p>We put every fee arrangement in writing before work starts, so you know the cost and how it is calculated. We will talk through the options on a first call.</p></details><details><summary>What do you need from me to review a claim?</summary><p>Send the policy declarations and appraisal condition, the carrier’s estimate and payment letters, any contractor estimates and your photos. That is usually enough for an initial read on whether appraisal fits.</p></details><details><summary>Can you serve as an umpire instead of a party appraiser?</summary><p>Yes, we can be considered for umpire roles when we have no connection to either party or appraiser. We do not act as both appraiser and umpire on the same claim.</p></details><details><summary>Do you give legal advice?</summary><p>No. We are an appraisal practice, not a law firm or a public adjuster, so questions about coverage, bad faith or legal deadlines should go to a Maine attorney.</p></details><details><summary>Do you work on island and rural properties?</summary><p>Yes, we work statewide across all 16 counties, including ferry-served islands and remote camps. Access logistics are planned into the inspection schedule.</p></details></div></article>
